 "caveats": [
  {
   "title": "§5-12 step 4 is broken in the manual, and the missing words are at the top of step 1",
   "text": "The printed step 4 ends 'If a failure is detected, check relay K5 and90.' -- and §5-12 step 1 opens with the orphan fragment 'MΩ resistor in R1.' before its real first sentence. Both were read off the rendered pages (1996 manual, printed pages 5-57 and 5-58), so this is the manual's own typesetting and not a text-extraction artefact. Read together the sentence is 'check relay K5 and the 90 MΩ resistor in R1', which is exactly what §2-169 describes and what the Rev 9 parts list confirms by giving R1 as RES,CERM,90M. The step text in this dataset is left as printed rather than repaired, so the evidence survives; the 100 MΩ probe point carries the repaired reading in its note.",
   "refs": [
    "K5",
    "R1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "R1 is a different part in the two parts lists",
   "text": "The 1996 Table 6-11 gives R1 as 'R-NET, THIN FILM, TESTED, FRIT', Fluke stock 103306, with no manufacturer columns and no ESD marking. The Rev 9 Table 6-11 gives it as 'RES,CERM,90M,+-.025%,0.75W,10 PPM', stock 783654, supply code 19647, part TF656R, and marks it ESD-sensitive. These are not the same part and the join on stock number correctly refused to merge them -- one of only two rows on this board that did. Both descriptions agree with §2-169's '90 MΩ film resistor (R1)', and the value R1 has to be is not in doubt; what the two revisions disagree about is which part Fluke shipped. The dataset carries the 1996 row as the part to order and the Rev 9 row as asBuilt.",
   "refs": [
    "R1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "R6: both parts lists say 110 kΩ, the schematic says 287 kΩ, and the theory agrees with the schematic",
   "text": "Table 6-11 gives R6 as RES,MF,110K,±1%,0.125W,100PPM in both revisions -- 1996 at stock 234708, Rev 9 at stock 719526 with Vishay/Dale CMF-55 1103F T-1, where '1103' is itself 110 kΩ. Figure 7-10 sheet 1, zone A6 beside K38, draws it as 'R6 287K MF'. §2-171 says the resistor's job is to make 'an opposite-polarity 60 µA current' from the -17 S supply to cancel the 60 µA the Current assembly puts on DAC OUT LO: 17 V / 287 kΩ = 59 µA, which is the figure the theory asks for, while 17 V / 110 kΩ = 155 µA, which would over-cancel by two and a half times. So the schematic and the theory agree against both parts lists. This dataset does NOT resolve it: the BOM shows what the parts lists print, because that is what an order will be filled from, and this caveat records that the board is very unlikely to contain a 110 kΩ part there. Measure R6 before replacing it.",
   "refs": [
    "R6",
    "K38"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The test point legend puts TP3 in the wrong zone",
   "text": "The legend box at sheet 1 zone C1 prints 'TP2 = OHMS SENSE LO (PG1A7)' and 'TP3 = OHMS OUT LO (PG1A8)'. On the sheet, TP3's flag is drawn to the RIGHT of TP2's, and columns run 8 down to 1 from left to right -- so TP3 cannot be in a higher-numbered column than TP2. Measured against the frame both flags fall in column 7, row A, so TP2's printed zone is right and TP3's is not. The signal names themselves are confirmed independently: TP2's flag sits on the OHMS SENSE LO net at P301 25A/25C and TP3's on OHMS OUT LO at 24A/24C, exactly as the legend names them. The other three entries (TP1 = B8, TP4 and TP5 = D8) agree with the geometry.",
   "refs": [
    "TP2",
    "TP3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Two of Figure 7-10's four sheets carry no circuit at all",
   "text": "Sheets 3 and 4 of drawing 5700A-1030 are the relay state tables -- 'OHMS MAIN ASSEMBLY RELAYS' and 'OHMS CAL ASSEMBLY RELAYS AND FETS' -- listing, for every instrument state and every calibration step, which relay is reset and which non-latching relay is energised. This is the 'relay chart' §5-12 step 2 tells you to use when a path fails, and it is the most useful thing on the board for tracing, but it is nothing but designators laid out in a grid. No component is placed on either sheet: every marker a reader finds there would be a table column header rather than a part. Sheets 1 and 2 carry the whole circuit between them.",
   "refs": []
  },
  {
   "title": "The photographed board is one revision newer than the manual documents",
   "text": "The Manual Status Information table at the front of Chapter 6 documents A10 as Fluke 761114 at revision level 100. The board in the photograph is silkscreened 'OHMS MAIN ASSY REV' with the field hand-inked 101, over etch '5700A-3030 REV 101 © 1987 FLUKE MADE IN USA'. The dataset records rev 101 because that is what is on the board. Nothing in the parts list is known to differ between the two levels; the R1 and R6 stock-number changes above are differences between the 1996 and Rev 9 manuals, which is a different axis.",
   "refs": []
  },
  {
   "title": "Fault code 3324's printed description is a copy of 3321's",
   "text": "§5-2 describes 3324, '100M Ohm Ratio Fault', as '10 V across the 10 MΩ string, 1 V across the 1 MΩ portion' -- word for word what it says for 3321, '10M Ohm Ratio Fault'. No 100 MΩ measurement is described anywhere in the code. The 100 MΩ value is two-wire and is R1 in series above the 10 MΩ tap, so a genuine 100 MΩ ratio test cannot be the same measurement as 3321's. The fault code entry keeps the printed text and says so in its verdict rather than inventing a corrected description.",
   "refs": [
    "R1",
    "K5"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The two parts lists describe H3-H6 differently",
   "text": "Both lists carry stock 838458 at quantity 4, so the join succeeded and the disagreement is not in the review report: the 1996 list calls it 'RIVET,S-TUB,OVAL,AL,.087,.343' and the Rev 9 list 'RIVET,S-TUB,OVAL,STL,.087,.343' -- aluminium against steel for the same stock number. It is a rivet holding the heat shield standoffs and nothing measurable depends on it, but it is a real disagreement between sources and is recorded here rather than quietly dropped. The dataset shows the 1996 description.",
   "refs": [
    "H3",
    "H4",
    "H5",
    "H6"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Table 5-2 is not carried as a table of its own",
   "text": "§5-12 step 3 says only 'repeat step 2 for the resistances in Table 5-2'. The table itself -- 19 Ω and 100 Ω and 190 Ω and 1 kΩ and 1.9 kΩ with their tolerances -- is printed between steps 3 and 4, and the procedure parser absorbs part of it into step 3's text and drops the rows whose lines carry no lower-case letter. So the step text shows two of the five rows. All five values are carried in full as probe points instead, each with its own tolerance and its citation to Table 5-2, which is where to read them. The reference-table slot in tools/assemble.py accepts only two fixed keys (paSupplySettings, currentLimit) and neither describes this table, so nothing was forced into it.",
   "refs": []
  },
  {
   "title": "What the curated study document got wrong",
   "text": "../MD/A10_Ohms_Main.md was used as a starting point and proved accurate on structure and on the R6 finding, but three of its numbers did not survive checking. It says the ESD-marked parts include 'all four resistance networks': in the 1996 list R1 carries no ESD mark at all (only MP7, U1, U2, U3, U5-U8, U9, U10, U11-U13, VR1, Z1, Z2 and Z3 do), and R1's mark exists only in the Rev 9 list, where it is a different part. It assigns Z3 the elements '17.1 M, 1.71 M, 171 k, 17.1 k, 1710, 171, 19': sheet 1 shows the last three (1710, 171, 19) inside Z1, not Z3, in the same package as the 1x low decades. And it describes VR1 as a 'supply clamp': sheet 1 draws it in series in the +17 S feed with its cathode at the rail, so it is a 3.3 V series drop rather than a shunt. Its 100 MΩ note also lists R1's 1996 stock number against the Rev 9 90 MΩ description, mixing the two revisions in one row.",
   "refs": [
    "R1",
    "VR1",
    "Z1",
    "Z3"
   ]
  }
 ],
